<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Adhemar of Le Puy</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">died 1098</span> · Also known as Adhemar of Monteil</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Adhemar of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/le-puy-en-velay">Le Puy</a>, also called Adhemar of Monteil, was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of Le Puy and the papal legate appointed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-urban-ii">Pope Urban II</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-crusade">First Crusade</a>. He travelled with the army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raymond-iv-of-toulouse">Raymond of Saint-Gilles</a>, count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toulouse">Toulouse</a>, and acted as spiritual leader and mediator among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a> princes. He died of disease at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a> in August 1098, before the capture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/adhemar-of-le-puy.jpg" alt="Adhemar of Le Puy" width="320" height="384" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Adh%C3%A9mar_de_Monteil_%C3%A0_Antioche.jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
